            <ArticleTitle>Spatial and Census Data to Evaluate Obese Persons and their Environment (SCOPE).</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>582-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5993/AJHB.39.4.15</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the built environment and its relationship to BMI for individuals in eastern Idaho.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Geospatial analyses were coupled to demographic data of adult individuals. ArcGIS Community Analyst was used to compare demographics relative to median BMI.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">For every kilometer increase in distance to prepared food sites, BMI went down by 1.3% and every kilometer increase in distance to green space, BMI went down by 0.8% (p &lt; .001). For every kilometer increase in distance to trails, BMI went up by 1.5%. No other built environment variables had a statistically significant association with BMI.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The distance to prepared foods and trails was associated with expected changes in BMI. Conversely, increased distance to green space was associated with a lower BMI.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
